#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105918 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105490 :question/id #uuid "4693b28f-df75-574e-8e49-bb89e162b107" :question/math-academy-id "e-15608" :question/problem "Let ![](images/e4/e477b14c9148f4132c747a358e1e99f76d40df85b82c66d6e7dd38581b33913c.png) and ![](images/b1/b17d86e3e395b2fd752f5529090c0a5e4e2fdce17642be5594bee98e8c47fed2.png) Find the scalar projection of $\\mathbf{a}$ onto $\\mathbf{b}.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula for the scalar projection, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{b}\\,a & =\\frac{a\\cdot b}{|\\,b\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{a}_{x}{b}_{x}+{a}_{y}{b}_{y}+{c}_{z}{b}_{z}}{\\sqrt{{b}_{x}^{2}+{b}_{y}^{2}+{b}_{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0\\cdot 2+0\\cdot (-4)+1\\cdot 0}{\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(-4)^{2}+{0}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(-4)^{2}+{0}^{2}}} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a51e317c-f6b3-5704-afb4-bdfc79f1176f" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106213 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1000e887-f114-51b9-b67a-d436889f9bb6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288151} {:answer/id #uuid "4f6cbdef-32ec-5499-a6bd-ce420ddec4fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288580} {:answer/id #uuid "812d7309-b400-5fd3-bd24-0d8abc7cac68" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288903} {:answer/id #uuid "a7847429-4b08-529a-a14c-c8cbd6af9359" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289142} {:answer/id #uuid "dfa64626-acf1-57f9-bd83-06342e759333" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289525}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4f6cbdef-32ec-5499-a6bd-ce420ddec4fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288580} :answer-field/id #uuid "2edfa40a-17b0-5eff-898c-e2c29a2c872d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289836}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "71a079d2-97d0-5455-a26c-8d71df225e2c" :question/math-academy-id "q-236734" :question/problem "Let $p=⟨-1,1,2⟩$ and $q=⟨4,x,1⟩.$ Given that the scalar projection of $p$ onto $q$ is zero, find the value of $x.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $p$ is a nonzero vector, its scalar projection onto another nonzero vector $q$ equals zero only when the vectors are perpendicular. \n\nSo, we must have\n\n$$\np⊥q\\,⟹\\,p\\cdot q=0.\n$$\n\nComputing the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p\\cdot q & ={p}_{x}\\cdot {q}_{x}+{p}_{y}\\cdot {q}_{y}+{p}_{z}\\cdot {q}_{z} \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot 4+1\\cdot x+2\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =x-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p\\cdot q & =0 \\\\ x-2 & =0 \\\\ x & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106214 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "32a9b8e6-e5c7-5386-b6a4-a0340f010993" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288364} {:answer/id #uuid "485ef75c-1b41-5e2a-a66c-0c07edb72c16" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288532} {:answer/id #uuid "61691320-cd84-5b8a-b32e-847e76c40d8b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288713} {:answer/id #uuid "e150dd45-0881-5820-a874-6959ab85bc53" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289535} {:answer/id #uuid "f232237e-47c0-523d-897d-f50021d1ccdb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289632}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "61691320-cd84-5b8a-b32e-847e76c40d8b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288713} :answer-field/id #uuid "71f1aa2c-7ea0-5d2e-810c-4873abf36f67" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289980}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f961e7bf-40d0-5b4f-86c7-b36ac1e85bd7" :question/math-academy-id "q-236735" :question/problem "Let $p=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]$ and $q=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ 4 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}].$ Given that the scalar projection of $p$ onto $q$ is $0,$ find the value of $x.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $p$ is a nonzero vector, its scalar projection onto another nonzero vector $q$ equals zero only when the vectors are perpendicular. \n\nSo, we must have\n\n$$\np⊥q\\,⟹\\,p\\cdot q=0.\n$$\n\nComputing the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p\\cdot q & ={p}_{x}\\cdot {q}_{x}+{p}_{y}\\cdot {q}_{y}+{p}_{z}\\cdot {q}_{z} \\\\  & =6\\cdot x+1\\cdot 4+4\\cdot (-4) \\\\  & =6x-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p\\cdot q & =0 \\\\ 6x-12 & =0 \\\\ 6x & =12 \\\\ x & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290486 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "641ccdd7-0572-5c4f-b7e6-d4afe84948d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288736} {:answer/id #uuid "66ccc376-8b86-58f9-b2ff-5c6b7268656c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288752} {:answer/id #uuid "72db54e1-ea6b-5dd6-a346-7ef3737b3912" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288831} {:answer/id #uuid "d8aca7b4-fb6f-58c2-83da-427e09c26a5a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289478} {:answer/id #uuid "ed0c240a-396a-521b-addd-33491e305d80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289599}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "72db54e1-ea6b-5dd6-a346-7ef3737b3912" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288831} :answer-field/id #uuid "d2d641b9-8ae0-5f17-b941-11db55cc78a9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290188}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a5d78299-26e4-51e8-b76f-fbcf41be9fda" :question/math-academy-id "q-237014" :question/problem "Let $p=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]$ and $q=[\\begin{aligned}15 \\\\ \\sqrt{2} \\\\ x\\end{aligned}].$ Given that the scalar projection of $p$ onto $q$ is $0,$ find the value of $x.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $p$ is a nonzero vector, its scalar projection onto another nonzero vector $q$ equals zero only when the vectors are perpendicular. \n\nSo, we must have\n\n$$\np⊥q\\,⟹\\,p\\cdot q=0.\n$$\n\nComputing the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p\\cdot q & ={p}_{x}\\cdot {q}_{x}+{p}_{y}\\cdot {q}_{y}+{p}_{z}\\cdot {q}_{z} \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot 15+0\\cdot \\sqrt{2}+5\\cdot x \\\\  & =5x-30.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p\\cdot q & =0 \\\\ 5x-30 & =0 \\\\ 5x & =30 \\\\ x & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290487 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f63989c-30f1-5c24-b29f-efbf4137d328" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288234} {:answer/id #uuid "1fcdfc3c-0b4f-56d0-8865-1174e6c456fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288238} {:answer/id #uuid "436014ad-b619-5cc4-b0db-f17722d1681a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288492} {:answer/id #uuid "45206803-f9d0-55fe-be31-cc4c00a72853" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288501} {:answer/id #uuid "92856ac6-8e97-51a2-9023-612b203bc678" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289016}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1f63989c-30f1-5c24-b29f-efbf4137d328" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288234} :answer-field/id #uuid "094c47e3-6adf-5882-8699-9b8ef4f4d74a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289746}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f879853f-8faf-5226-ac51-81b9eb8102b2" :question/math-academy-id "q-237015" :question/problem "Let $p=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ x \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ and $q=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].$ Given that the scalar projection of $p$ onto $q$ is $0,$ find the value of $x.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $p$ is a nonzero vector, its scalar projection onto another nonzero vector $q$ equals zero only when the vectors are perpendicular. \n\nSo, we must have\n\n$$\np⊥q\\,⟹\\,p\\cdot q=0.\n$$\n\nComputing the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p\\cdot q & ={p}_{x}\\cdot {q}_{x}+{p}_{y}\\cdot {q}_{y}+{p}_{z}\\cdot {q}_{z} \\\\  & =5\\cdot 4+x\\cdot (-2)+2\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =14-2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p\\cdot q & =0 \\\\ 14-2x & =0 \\\\ 2x & =14 \\\\ x & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290488 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b700fff-7350-5a76-9d33-c6381d7b4af9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288117} {:answer/id #uuid "1c148563-2d95-5d3e-a347-48d7c955421a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288212} {:answer/id #uuid "2a0223ed-9e09-5f4d-8cc1-702460540107" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #